\details{
\strong{What counts as hidden} is decided by two exact rules from Unicode's own
data, not by a guess:
\itemize{
\item \strong{A variation selector is hidden when it cannot be part of any sequence
Unicode defines}: when it follows another selector (no defined sequence
has two), when it is an ideographic selector (\code{U+E0100} to \code{U+E01EF})
that does not follow a CJK ideograph, or when it is one of \code{U+FE00} to
\code{U+FE0D} and the pair is not a standardized variation sequence. A single
\code{U+FE0E} or \code{U+FE0F} is never hidden, wherever it stands, because
redundant presentation selectors are common in ordinary text and one
carries at most one byte.
\item \strong{A tag character is hidden unless it is inside a well-formed flag tag
sequence}: \verb{U+1F3F4}, tag letters spelling a subdivision code, then
\code{U+E007F}. The England, Scotland and Wales flags are untouched, and so is
a well-formed subdivision flag the catalogue does not list.
}

So Japanese text spelled with ideographic variation selectors, the
mathematical and Mongolian variants Unicode standardizes, every keycap,
every catalogue spelling and every flag report nothing. Zero-width spaces,
joiners and non-joiners are deliberately not inspected: they are not emoji
mechanisms, and Persian, the Indic scripts, Thai and Khmer need them. Use a
general Unicode-security tool for those.

\strong{Decoding} covers the two published encodings only: variation selectors
as bytes (\code{U+FE00} to \code{U+FE0F} as 0 to 15, \code{U+E0100} to \code{U+E01EF} as 16 to
255, the scheme of Butler, 2025), accepted only when the bytes are valid
UTF-8; and tag characters \code{U+E0020} to \code{U+E007E} as ASCII. Each contiguous
run of hidden code points is decoded on its own and the runs are joined by
\code{" | "}. A row with any run in neither encoding gets an \code{NA} payload and
\code{.emoji_hidden_decoded = FALSE}; it is still counted.

\strong{The payload is attacker text, returned as data.} Do not paste it into a
model. Every C0 and C1 control character and every bidirectional control
in it is replaced by a visible \verb{<U+XXXX>} marker, so printing the result in
a console or a knitted report cannot recolour or rewrite the terminal or
reorder what a reader sees.

